  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 AM EDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nToday-Tonight...Atlantic ridge axis north of the area will being to \nshift southward today. However, surface winds will remain mostly \nonshore out of the southeast, becoming east-southeast and increasing \nto 10-15 mph this afternoon behind the inland moving east coast sea \nbreeze. Mid level ridge will begin to nudge in from the east, \nwith drier air aloft moving in and dropping PW values to 1.8-2.0\".\nThis will lead to decreasing coverage of showers and storms today\n(rain chances 30-50%). CAM guidance shows varying solutions for \nconvective formation today, but overall trends favor isolated to \nscattered showers and potentially a storm or two moving onshore \nalong the coast (mainly south of the Cape) this morning. Isolated \nto scattered showers and storms will then develop along the sea \nbreeze as it shifts inland, with greatest coverage focusing across\nthe interior, west of I-95 into the afternoon/early evening. The \nincrease in drier air will lead to rising DCAPE values, ranging \nfrom 900-1200 J/kg. This will favor some stronger storms \ndeveloping today, with the main threats being strong wind gusts to\n40-50 mph, frequent lightning strikes and locally heavy rainfall \nof 1-3\" as steering winds will be weak (10 knots or less) out of \nthe S/SE. \n\nIt will remain hot and humid, with highs continuing in the low 90s \nfor most locations, but may see mid 90s for some spots near to NW of \nI-4 this afternoon. Peak heat index values will continue to be \naround 102-107F. If planning outdoor activities today, make sure to \nstay hydrated and take frequent breaks in the shade or A/C. Also \nnever leave children or pets unattended in a vehicle for any period \nof time. If headed to the beach for heat relief today, be aware that \na High Risk of rip currents continues at area beaches. If entering \nthe surf, only do so near a lifeguard.  \n\nMonday-Saturday...Not a huge change in the weather pattern this \nweek, as ridge aloft moves in and holds across the area contributing \nto hot conditions and below normal shower and storm chances. \nIsolated to scattered showers will still be possible into Monday \nafternoon/evening. Greatest rain chances (up to 30-40%) will exist \nacross inland portions of the Treasure Coast and Okeechobee County, \nwith slight rain chances (~20%) elsewhere. For the remainder of the \nweek, rain chances currently remain quite limited around 20% or less \nas mid level drier air continues to move into the region and ridge \naloft remains in place. A gradual rise in rain chances is possible\ninto the weekend, but coverage still looks to be overall below \nnormal. Given the increase in drier air aloft, while storm \ncoverage remains below normal, any storms that do manage to form \nwill have the potential to become strong, producing strong wind \ngusts of 40-50 mph and frequent lightning strikes. Weak steering \nwinds will also allow for heavy downpours from some storms, \nproducing locally heavy rainfall of 1-2\" each day. \n\nThese drier than normal conditions will allow for rising heat \nimpacts through the week as little relief will be found from \nafternoon convection, and afternoon highs will reach the low to mid \n90s each day. Peak heat index values will easily reach 102-107F, and \nwill still have to monitor the need for Heat Advisories, especially \nfrom Tuesday onward as portions of the area may see heat index \nvalues rise to 108-110F. Warm and muggy overnight temps in the mid \nto upper 70s will also amplify the overall heat impacts, with \nwidespread Moderate to Major HeatRisk forecast for much of the\narea each day. Continuing to follow heat safety practices will \nremain important as we go through the week.",
